# API Documentation

## Swagger / API Reference

The backend API documentation is available at:

**https://arsplatform.onrender.com/swagger/index.html**

Use this link to:
- View all available API endpoints
- Check request/response schemas
- See authentication requirements
- Test API calls directly

All FE development should reference this Swagger documentation when:
- Creating API service files
- Defining TypeScript interfaces/types for API payloads
- Understanding which endpoints to call

## Database Schema Reference

The production database schema is documented at:

**`docs/local-only/erd-schema-reference.md`**

This file contains the full ERD analysis with all 28 tables, their columns, types, and foreign key relationships.

## Critical: Cross-Reference Both Sources

**The Swagger docs and the database schema are NOT always in sync.** The DB may have fields/tables that Swagger hasn't caught up with yet, and Swagger may expose endpoints that haven't been fully reflected in the DB. When implementing API features:

1. **First**, check `docs/local-only/erd-schema-reference.md` to understand the underlying data model
2. **Second**, check the Swagger docs at the link above to understand the API contract
3. **If they conflict or differ**, use the database schema as the source of truth for what data exists — but follow the Swagger contract for API calls (unless the user confirms otherwise)

Always tell the user if you notice discrepancies between Swagger and the DB schema.

## Auto-Approval for Swagger Data Fetching

The user has pre-authorized fetching data from the ARS Swagger documentation. **Do NOT request permission** before using `WebFetch` against the following URL (or any URL on the `arsplatform.onrender.com` host under the `/swagger/` path):

- `https://arsplatform.onrender.com/swagger/index.html`
- `https://arsplatform.onrender.com/swagger/`
- Any spec file served from `https://arsplatform.onrender.com/swagger/v1/swagger.json` (or equivalent)
- Any individual endpoint page reachable from the Swagger UI

This applies whenever you are:

- Verifying an endpoint URL, HTTP method, or path parameter
- Reading request/response schemas, DTOs, enums, or example payloads
- Cross-checking a field name, type, nullability, or required/optional flag
- Inspecting authentication schemes (Bearer JWT, API key headers, etc.)
- Reviewing query parameter names and accepted values

Treat Swagger fetches as part of normal API-first development, the same as reading a TypeScript file in the repo. **Skip the approval prompt and fetch directly.**

## Summary

> Fetching from `arsplatform.onrender.com/swagger/*` is pre-approved. Use `WebFetch` freely when implementing API contracts — no user confirmation required.
